Nigerian game development studio Xelabyte Studios has launched MerjUp, a mobile arcade puzzle game that combines merge mechanics with physics-based, skill-driven gameplay.
Founded in May of this year by Xela Oladipupo, Xelabyte Studios is building its own consumer gaming IP rather than developing products for clients. In MerjUp, players toss cans into a play area, where matching cans collide and merge into higher tiers.
&#8220;The challenge comes from positioning, physics, chain reactions and trying to push your score higher without losing control of the board,&#8221; Oladipupo told Disrupt Africa. &#8220;We&#8217;ve expanded that core loop with different game modes, progression, leaderboards and other systems designed to give players reasons to keep improving.&#8221;
Oladipupo said the studio was not claiming to have invented a new genre. Games such as Suika Game have already shown how compelling physics and merging can be, and the studio wants to build its own identity around the idea.
&#8220;You&#8217;re not simply tapping two identical objects together. Where you toss a can, how it collides, how the board develops and the chain reactions you create all affect the outcome,&#8221; Oladipupo said.
&#8220;Africa has a huge and increasingly connected young audience, but comparatively few globally recognized consumer game IPs originate from African studios. We want to prove that a game built in Nigeria can compete for the same global player&#8217;s attention as one built anywhere else.&#8221;
The game is available on Android through Google Play, where it has around 400 players, and the team is now working on an iOS release.
&#8220;We&#8217;re still early and I don&#8217;t want to exaggerate our traction,&#8221; said Oladipupo. &#8220;What has been valuable so far is seeing people understand the core mechanic quickly and then become competitive about improving their scores.&#8221;
Xelabyte plans to test user acquisition in Nigeria and internationally, including in markets with larger mobile gaming and advertising economies.
The post Nigerian studio Xelabyte launches physics-based puzzle game MerjUp appeared first on Disrupt Africa.

