items.content:
Four-time world champion Max Verstappen secured his and Red Bull's first win this year with an imperious drive Sunday in chaotic conditions in a storm-delayed Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia.
Nine years after winning the last F1 race at the Sepang International Circuit, the 29-year-old Dutchman came through a wild afternoon of accidents and incidents for his 72nd victory and first since Abu Dhabi last year.
In a race of shifting tyre strategies, he came home 2.307 seconds ahead of championship leader Kimi Antonelli of Mercedes and seven-time champion Lewis Hamilton who took third after a thrilling recovery drive.&nbsp;
His Ferrari teammate Charles Leclerc was fourth, having fallen to last place in the early laps, ahead of Isack Hadjar in the second Red Bull, McLaren's Oscar Piastri and the Racing Bull of Liam Lawson.&nbsp;
Two-time champion Fernando Alonso finished a season-best eighth for Aston Martin ahead of world champion Lando Norris and Racing Bull rookie Arvid Lindblad.
The result extended Antonelli's lead to 84 points ahead of teammate George Russell, who retired after a late power failure under a safety car.&nbsp;
&quot;It is really nice to win after the year we've had and all the progression we've made,&quot; said a delighted Verstappen.
&quot;It is super-nice to win here. It was very hectic with a lot of different things, but we made the right call with the inters (intermediate tyres).&quot;
Antonelli congratulated him and added: &quot;It was the maximum we could have done. We struggled, but I am happy to come away with P2.&quot;
'Tough race'
Hamilton was greeted by a roar from the big crowd.&nbsp;
&quot;What a tough race that was,&quot; he said.&nbsp;
&quot;I was more than a minute back after we made the wrong call on tyres at the start and everyone was pulling away.&quot;
The race had followed a protracted 100-minute delay following a tropical rainstorm, and began with half the field on intermediate tyres.&nbsp;
Both Mercedes promptly swamped Verstappen, Antonelli taking the lead in turn one, followed by Russell.&nbsp;
Hamilton, on soft slicks, had no grip and tumbled down the order from second on the grid as it became clear that the conditions remained treacherous.
It all changed on lap nine when Valtteri Bottas slid off into the gravel and the safety car was deployed, bringing nearly everyone in for dry tyres with Antonelli taking mediums and Verstappen softs.&nbsp;
Hamilton stayed out and upon the resumption Verstappen pounced to sweep round Antonelli and regain a lead he would not relinquish.&nbsp;
Hamilton continued his recovery, slicing through the field and by lap 23 had dispensed with Lawson to take fifth.
He closed up within six seconds of the Frenchman before the pair pitted on lap 31. &quot;This tyre feels terrible,&quot; said Hamilton of his new mediums.
A late virtual and then full safety car saw everyone pit for fresh softs.&nbsp;
Russell's retirement enabled Hamilton to get on to the podium when he passed Hadjar in the final four-lap dash to the chequered flag.

The Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MoFA) on Saturday summoned the Indian Charg&eacute; d'Affaires and lodged a strong protest over the killing of two Pakistani civilians by personnel of the Indian Border Security Force (BSF) in the Bedian Sector along the International Border on Friday.
&quot;The BSF personnel resorted to unprovoked lethal force against unarmed civilians. Such use of force was wholly unjustified and grossly disproportionate, resulting in the tragic loss of two precious lives. Pakistan categorically rejects any attempt to mischaracterise the deceased as 'infiltrators,&quot; said a statement issued by MoFA.
The statement came a day after two Pakistani civilians were killed, and another was injured after Indian BSF personnel opened fire near the Pakistan-India international border in Kasur. The incident reportedly occurred when three residents from Ladhar village moved a few steps beyond a border pillar, upon which BSF personnel opened fire on the men without warning.
According to MoFA, the incident amounted to a serious violation of fundamental human rights, including the right to life, and raised concerns over the protection of civilians under international law.
It noted that &quot;the Indian side was called upon to conduct an immediate, transparent and credible investigation into the incident, take appropriate disciplinary and legal action against those found responsible, and formally communicate the findings and action taken to the Government of Pakistan.&quot;
Further, it added that &quot;India was further urged to ensure respect for fundamental human rights and international law, strictly observe relevant bilateral arrangements and established border procedures, exercise maximum restraint in dealing with such situations, and take effective measures to prevent the recurrence of such incidents.&quot;

